From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 19:53:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/25] runtime health: correct queueing and scheduler lag

Describe per-bucket queued values as observed queueing rather than proof that a
configured function concurrency limit was reached. A transient queued sample
can be the normal interval between outstanding accounting and asynchronous
permit acquisition.

Track scheduled-function ready-state lag in a dedicated 15-second store with
240 buckets and its own lock, while leaving the generic UDF metric store
unchanged. Record every successful queue decision independently of the existing
30-second structured-log coalescing, including refreshed queue heads while an
executor is full, so cancellation or replacement cannot leave stale state for
dashboard extrapolation.

Normalize samples to their source-bucket starts and reconstruct lag at requested
timestamps. Seed a query window from retained earlier state when scheduler
errors prevent newer observations, and reset the dedicated timeline after a
backward wall-clock step so pre-jump timestamps cannot manufacture lag or
recovery.

Record an unlabeled direct admission-lag histogram for each ordinary scheduled
job admission attempt. Anchor it at the due-time decision and add monotonic scan
elapsed so wall-clock movement during dispatch does not distort the sample.
Keep the legacy ordinary and cron execution-lag histograms as sampled
ready-queue measurements.

Request and refresh the one-hour scheduler view at 15-second resolution,
include the current aligned bucket, preserve missing samples as chart gaps, and
format sub-second, second, and minute values explicitly. Align deployment
markers, stories, operator documentation, and generated screenshots with those
semantics.

Add focused scheduler, MetricStore, rollback, retained-state, dashboard, and
queueing regressions plus one operator README for the combined adoption unit.
